\label{ch:poly-types}

Parametric polymorphism---the ability to write functions that operate uniformly over all types---is one of the most powerful abstractions in programming language design. The theoretical foundation for this abstraction is \emph{System~F}, the polymorphic lambda calculus introduced independently by Girard~\cite{girard1972} in the context of proof theory and by Reynolds~\cite{reynolds1972} in the context of data types. This chapter develops System~F in depth, explores its extensions, and examines the challenges of implementing polymorphic type systems in practice.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 45.1
%% ============================================================
\section{System F: Syntax and Semantics}\label{sec:system-f}

System~F extends the simply-typed lambda calculus with universal quantification over types. It is a pure, strongly normalizing calculus---every well-typed term terminates---yet it is expressive enough to encode all definable functions on natural numbers (the System~F numerals).

\subsection{Syntax}\label{sec:system-f-syntax}

\begin{grammar}{System F}
  \production{\tau}{\alpha \mid \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \mid \forall \alpha.\, \tau} \\
  \production{e}{x \mid \lambda x : \tau.\, e \mid e_1\; e_2 \mid \Lambda \alpha.\, e \mid e\; [\tau]}
\end{grammar}

Types $\tau$ include type variables $\alpha$, function types $\tau_1 \to \tau_2$, and universal types $\forall \alpha.\, \tau$. Expressions $e$ include variables, abstractions, applications, \emph{type abstractions} $\Lambda \alpha.\, e$, and \emph{type applications} $e\; [\tau]$.

\subsection{Typing Rules}\label{sec:system-f-typing}

\begin{typerules}{System F}
  \typeaxiom{Var}{\Gamma, x : \tau \vdash x : \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs}{\Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e : \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash \lambda x : \tau_1.\, e : \tau_1 \to \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \quad \Gamma \vdash e_2 : \tau_1}
            {\Gamma \vdash e_1\; e_2 : \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{TAbs}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau \quad \alpha \notin \mathrm{fv}(\Gamma)}
            {\Gamma \vdash \Lambda \alpha.\, e : \forall \alpha.\, \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{TApp}{\Gamma \vdash e : \forall \alpha.\, \tau}
            {\Gamma \vdash e\; [\tau'] : \tau[\tau'/\alpha]}
\end{typerules}

\subsection{Operational Semantics}\label{sec:system-f-semantics}

The operational semantics of System~F extends beta-reduction with type-level computation:

\begin{semrules}{System F Reduction}
  \semruleaxiom{(\lambda x : \tau.\, e)\; v \longrightarrow e[v/x]}{Beta-value} \\[6pt]
  \semruleaxiom{(\Lambda \alpha.\, e)\; [\tau] \longrightarrow e}{Type-beta} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{e_1 \longrightarrow e_1'}{e_1\; e_2 \longrightarrow e_1'\; e_2}{App-left} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{e_2 \longrightarrow e_2'}{v\; e_2 \longrightarrow v\; e_2'}{App-right} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{e \longrightarrow e'}{e\; [\tau] \longrightarrow e'\; [\tau]}{TApp}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Properties}\label{sec:system-f-properties}

\begin{theorem}[Type Soundness]\label{thm:system-f-sound}
If $\vdash e : \tau$ and $e \longrightarrow e'$, then $\vdash e' : \tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strong Normalization]\label{thm:system-f-sn}
Every well-typed term in System~F is strongly normalizing: every reduction sequence terminates.
\end{theorem}

The strong normalization theorem, proved by Girard~\cite{girard1972} using the method of \emph{reducibility candidates} (a semantic variant of Tait's computability method), is one of the deepest results in type theory. It implies that System~F is a total language: every program terminates.

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The proof defines a semantic interpretation of types as sets of terms (the \emph{reducibility candidates}) and shows that every well-typed term belongs to the interpretation of its type. The interpretation of $\forall \alpha.\, \tau$ is the intersection of the interpretations of $\tau[\sigma/\alpha]$ over all reducibility candidates $\sigma$. The key lemma is that this intersection is itself a reducibility candidate, which requires a careful closure argument. \qed
\end{proof}

