\section{Girard--Reynolds Polymorphism}\label{sec:girard-reynolds}

The polymorphism of System~F is called \emph{parametric} because polymorphic functions must behave uniformly at all types. This uniformity, formalized by Reynolds~\cite{reynolds1983} as \emph{parametricity}, has profound consequences.

\subsection{The Parametricity Theorem}\label{sec:parametricity}

Reynolds' parametricity theorem states that every polymorphic function in System~F satisfies a \emph{relational interpretation}: it maps related inputs to related outputs, for every relation between types.

\begin{theorem}[Parametricity]\label{thm:parametricity}
If $\vdash e : \tau$, then for every relation $R$ between type interpretations, $(e, e) \in \mathcal{R}[\![\tau]\!]_R$, where $\mathcal{R}[\![\cdot]\!]$ is the relational interpretation of types.
\end{theorem}

The relational interpretation is defined as:
\begin{align*}
  \mathcal{R}[\![\alpha]\!]_R &= R(\alpha) \\
  \mathcal{R}[\![\tau_1 \to \tau_2]\!]_R &= \{(f, g) \mid \forall (a, b) \in \mathcal{R}[\![\tau_1]\!]_R.\, (f(a), g(b)) \in \mathcal{R}[\![\tau_2]\!]_R\} \\
  \mathcal{R}[\![\forall \alpha.\, \tau]\!]_R &= \{(a, b) \mid \forall \text{types } A, B, \forall R \subseteq A \times B.\, (a, b) \in \mathcal{R}[\![\tau]\!]_R\}
\end{align*}

\subsection{Consequences of Parametricity}\label{sec:parametricity-consequences}

Parametricity yields \emph{free theorems}: properties that hold for every term of a given polymorphic type, without examining the term's definition.

\begin{example}[Free theorem for map]\label{ex:free-theorem-map}
The type $\forall \alpha.\, \forall \beta.\, (\alpha \to \beta) \to [\alpha] \to [\beta]$ (encoded in System~F) guarantees that any function $f$ of this type satisfies:
\[
  \forall g, h, xs.\, f\; (h \circ g)\; xs = \mathtt{map}\; h\; (f\; g\; xs)
\]
This is a \emph{free theorem}: it holds for every parametrically polymorphic function of the given type, regardless of its implementation.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Free theorem for fold]\label{ex:free-theorem-fold}
Any function of type $\forall \alpha.\, (\alpha \to \alpha \to \alpha) \to \alpha \to [\alpha] \to \alpha$ must satisfy certain fusion laws. In particular, it must be a fold: its behavior is completely determined by its behavior on $\mathtt{nil}$ and $\mathtt{cons}$.
\end{example}

