\section{Rank-N Polymorphism}\label{sec:rank-n}

The \emph{rank} of a polymorphic type measures the nesting depth of universal quantifiers to the left of function arrows.

\begin{definition}[Rank]\label{def:rank}
The \emph{rank} of a type is defined recursively:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathrm{rank}(\tau) = 0$ if $\tau$ is a monotype (no $\forall$).
  \item $\mathrm{rank}(\forall \alpha.\, \tau) = \max(1, \mathrm{rank}(\tau))$.
  \item $\mathrm{rank}(\tau_1 \to \tau_2) = \max(\mathrm{rank}(\tau_1) + 1, \mathrm{rank}(\tau_2))$ if $\tau_1$ is not a monotype; otherwise $\mathrm{rank}(\tau_2)$.
\end{itemize}
A type has \emph{rank $N$} if its rank is $N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Examples of polymorphic types by rank}\label{tab:rank-examples}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Rank} \\
\hline
$\mathrm{int} \to \mathrm{int}$ & 0 (monotype) \\
$\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha$ & 1 (prenex / HM) \\
$(\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha) \to \mathrm{int}$ & 2 \\
$(\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha) \to (\forall \beta.\, \beta \to \beta)$ & 2 \\
$((\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha) \to \mathrm{int}) \to \mathrm{int}$ & 3 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Rank-1 and Hindley--Milner}\label{sec:rank-1}

Rank-1 types (also called \emph{prenex} types) are those where all quantifiers appear at the outermost level. The HM system infers exactly the rank-1 types: every inferred type is of the form $\forall \vec{\alpha}.\, \tau$ where $\tau$ is a monotype.

\subsection{Rank-2 Polymorphism}\label{sec:rank-2}

Rank-2 types allow universal quantification in the left argument of a single function arrow. Type checking (not inference) for rank-2 types is decidable:

\begin{example}[Rank-2 type]\label{ex:rank-2}
The function $\mathtt{apply} = \lambda f.\, \lambda x.\, f\; x$ can be given the rank-2 type:
\[
  (\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha) \to \forall \beta.\, \beta \to \beta
\]
This type says that $\mathtt{apply}$ takes a polymorphic function (one that works at all types) and returns a polymorphic function. The argument $f$ must be usable at any type, which is a stronger requirement than HM can express.
\end{example}

\subsection{Rank-N and Undecidability}\label{sec:rank-n-undecidable}

Type inference for rank-$N$ polymorphism ($N \geq 2$) is undecidable~\cite{wells1999}. However, type \emph{checking} (given a fully annotated term, verify its type) is decidable for any fixed rank. This is the basis of GHC's \texttt{RankNTypes} extension: the programmer provides type annotations at key points, and the checker verifies the types.

