\section{Impredicativity}\label{sec:impredicativity}

System~F is \emph{impredicative}: a polymorphic type can be instantiated with any type, including another polymorphic type. This is in contrast to \emph{predicative} systems, where instantiation is restricted to avoid self-reference.

\subsection{Impredicative Instantiation}\label{sec:impredicative-inst}

\begin{example}[Impredicative instantiation]\label{ex:impredicative}
The identity function $\mathtt{id} = \Lambda \alpha.\, \lambda x : \alpha.\, x$ has type $\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha$. In System~F, we can instantiate $\alpha$ with the polymorphic type $\forall \beta.\, \beta \to \beta$:
\[
  \mathtt{id}\; [\forall \beta.\, \beta \to \beta] : (\forall \beta.\, \beta \to \beta) \to (\forall \beta.\, \beta \to \beta)
\]
This is an impredicative instantiation: the type variable $\alpha$ is instantiated with a type that itself contains a quantifier.
\end{example}

\subsection{Predicative Variants}\label{sec:predicative}

Predicative systems stratify types into \emph{universes} $\mathcal{U}_0 : \mathcal{U}_1 : \mathcal{U}_2 : \cdots$ and restrict quantification: $\forall \alpha : \mathcal{U}_i.\, \tau$ lives in $\mathcal{U}_{i+1}$. This avoids certain paradoxes but limits expressiveness.

The Coq proof assistant uses a predicative hierarchy of universes, while Agda (by default) is impredicative for $\mathrm{Prop}$ but predicative for $\mathrm{Set}$.

\subsection{Implementation Challenges}\label{sec:impredicative-impl}

Impredicativity poses significant challenges for type inference. The problem is that when checking an application $e_1\; e_2$, the type checker must decide whether to instantiate a polymorphic type in $e_1$ with a monotype or a polytype. Without annotations, this choice is undecidable.

GHC's \texttt{ImpredicativeTypes} extension uses a heuristic approach: it attempts impredicative instantiation only when the expected type is known (from bidirectional checking). This works for many common cases but can produce confusing errors in others.

\begin{insight}
The tension between impredicativity and inference is fundamental. System~F's impredicativity is essential for encoding data types (via Church encoding) and for the parametricity theorems. But it makes type inference undecidable. The practical resolution is to restrict inference to a predicative fragment (as in HM) and require annotations for impredicative uses.
\end{insight}

