\section{Implementation Challenges}\label{sec:poly-impl}

Implementing a polymorphic type system involves navigating several deep challenges.

\subsection{Type Representation}\label{sec:type-rep}

Polymorphic types require a representation that supports:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type variable binding.} The scope of $\forall \alpha$ must be tracked to avoid variable capture.
  \item \textbf{Type substitution.} Substituting a type for a variable must respect binding structure.
  \item \textbf{Type equality.} Two types are equal if they are $\alpha$-equivalent (identical up to renaming of bound variables).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Type Checking Algorithm}\label{sec:poly-checking}

Type checking for System~F (with annotations) is straightforward:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Type Checking for System F}\label{alg:system-f-check}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Check}{$\Gamma, e, \tau$}
  \State \textbf{case} $e$ \textbf{of}
  \State \quad $\lambda x : \tau_1.\, e'$:
    \State \quad \textbf{require} $\tau = \tau_1 \to \tau_2$
    \State \quad \Return $\mathrm{Check}(\Gamma[x : \tau_1], e', \tau_2)$
  \State \quad $\Lambda \alpha.\, e'$:
    \State \quad \textbf{require} $\tau = \forall \alpha.\, \tau'$
    \State \quad \Return $\mathrm{Check}(\Gamma, e', \tau')$
  \State \quad $e_1\; e_2$:
    \State \quad $\tau_1 \gets \mathrm{Infer}(\Gamma, e_1)$
    \State \quad \textbf{require} $\tau_1 = \tau_2' \to \tau$ for some $\tau_2'$
    \State \quad \Return $\mathrm{Check}(\Gamma, e_2, \tau_2')$
  \State \quad $e'\; [\tau']$:
    \State \quad $\sigma \gets \mathrm{Infer}(\Gamma, e')$
    \State \quad \textbf{require} $\sigma = \forall \alpha.\, \tau''$
    \State \quad \Return $\tau''[\tau'/\alpha] = \tau$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Avoiding Capture During System F Type Application}

Checking an explicit type application reduces to substituting the
argument type for the variable bound by the function's leading
universal quantifier. Under an inner quantifier, naive replacement
can silently turn a free variable of the argument into a bound one.
The following helper operates on finite named System~F types with
variable, arrow, and universal nodes. Its inputs are the body $\tau$,
the variable $a$ to replace, and the argument type $\sigma$.
Compute $F$ once as the free names of $\sigma$. When renaming a binder,
choose a name not used anywhere in either input or in the surrounding
context, and rename only occurrences bound by that binder.

\begin{sudocode}
\Function{Substitute}{$\tau,a,\sigma,F$}
  \If{$\tau$ is a variable $b$}
    \State \Return $\sigma$ if $a=b$, otherwise $\tau$
  \ElsIf{$\tau$ is an arrow $u\to v$}
    \State $u'\gets\Call{Substitute}{u,a,\sigma,F}$
    \State $v'\gets\Call{Substitute}{v,a,\sigma,F}$
    \State \Return $u'\to v'$
  \EndIf
  \State decompose $\tau$ as $\forall b.\,u$
  \If{$b=a$}
    \State \Return $\tau$ \Comment{Binder shadows the target}
  \EndIf
  \If{$b\in F$}
    \State $(b,u)\gets$ alpha-rename this binder \Comment{Keep $F$ free}
  \EndIf
  \State $u'\gets\Call{Substitute}{u,a,\sigma,F}$
  \State \Return $\forall b.\,u'$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

Suppose a term has type $\forall a.\forall b.\,a\to b$ in a
context that includes a type variable named $b$. Applying the term
to that ambient $b$ substitutes into $\forall b.\,a\to b$. Since
$F=\{b\}$, rename the inner binder to $c$ before descending. The
result is $\forall c.\,b\to c$, not $\forall b.\,b\to b$. The latter
would incorrectly turn a function whose domain has already been fixed
into one polymorphic in both its domain and its range.

The invariant is that every free occurrence inserted from $\sigma$
remains free relative to the binders traversed by the substitution.
In particular, a binder with the same name as $a$ stops traversal
even when $a$ also occurs free in $\sigma$. Recursive descent
terminates on the original type's structure; it never recurses
into an inserted argument. Repeated local renaming can rescan
large subtrees, so an implementation can instead use unique binder
identities or convert to indices. Tests should compare outputs
up to alpha-equivalence, since the fresh name itself is arbitrary.

\subsection{Compiling Polymorphism}\label{sec:compiling-poly}

There are two main strategies for compiling polymorphic programs:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type erasure.} All type information is removed at compile time. Polymorphic functions are compiled once, and type applications are no-ops. This is used by OCaml and (mostly) by GHC.
  \item \textbf{Type passing / monomorphization.} Each type instantiation generates a specialized copy of the polymorphic function. This is used by Rust, C++ templates, and (partially) by GHC for performance-critical code.
\end{itemize}

