\subsection{Dictionary Passing for Type Classes}\label{sec:dict-passing}

When System~F is used as a compilation target for type classes (see \cref{ch:hm-inference}), polymorphic functions receive an additional \emph{dictionary} argument containing the implementations of the type class methods:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source (Haskell):
  map :: (a -> b) -> [a] -> [b]

  -- Compiled (System F with dictionaries):
  map :: forall a b. Dict (Functor []) -> (a -> b) -> [a] -> [b]
\end{verbatim}

The dictionary is a record of function pointers, one for each method in the type class. This is the standard compilation strategy for type classes, used by GHC, and it reduces ad hoc polymorphism to parametric polymorphism plus record passing.

\begin{historical}
System~F was discovered independently by Jean-Yves Girard~\cite{girard1972}, who was studying the Curry--Howard correspondence for second-order propositional logic, and John Reynolds~\cite{reynolds1972}, who was studying the semantics of data abstraction. Girard proved its strong normalization property; Reynolds introduced the notion of parametricity. The connection between these two views---System~F as a logic and as a programming language---is one of the most beautiful ideas in computer science. The study of impredicativity, rank restrictions, and higher-kinded types has continued to shape the design of modern languages, from Haskell to Rust to Scala.
\end{historical}

