\section{Advanced Properties of System F}\label{sec:system-f-advanced}

System~F possesses several deep theoretical properties that have shaped the development of programming language theory.

\subsection{Strong Normalization via Reducibility}\label{sec:reducibility}

Girard's proof of strong normalization uses the method of \emph{reducibility candidates}, a semantic technique that assigns to each type a set of terms satisfying certain closure properties.

\begin{definition}[Reducibility Candidate]\label{def:reducibility}
A set $R$ of terms is a \emph{reducibility candidate} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Every term in $R$ is strongly normalizing.
  \item If $t \in R$ and $t \longrightarrow t'$, then $t' \in R$ (closure under reduction).
  \item If $t$ is neutral (a variable or application) and for every reducibility candidate $S$, $t\; s \in R$ for all $s \in S$, then $t \in R$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The interpretation of types is then:
\begin{align*}
  \llbracket \alpha \rrbracket_\rho &= \rho(\alpha) \\
  \llbracket \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \rrbracket_\rho &= \{t \mid \forall u \in \llbracket \tau_1 \rrbracket_\rho.\, t\; u \in \llbracket \tau_2 \rrbracket_\rho\} \\
  \llbracket \forall \alpha.\, \tau \rrbracket_\rho &= \bigcap_{R \in \mathcal{CR}} \llbracket \tau \rrbracket_{\rho[\alpha \mapsto R]}
\end{align*}

where $\mathcal{CR}$ is the set of all reducibility candidates.

\begin{theorem}[Adequacy]\label{thm:adequacy}
If $\vdash e : \tau$, then $e \in \llbracket \tau \rrbracket_\rho$ for every environment $\rho$.
\end{theorem}

Since every reducibility candidate contains only strongly normalizing terms, adequacy implies that every well-typed term is strongly normalizing.

\subsection{Impredicativity and Data Encoding}\label{sec:impredicative-encoding}

System~F's impredicativity enables the encoding of all algebraic data types via \emph{Church encoding}:

\begin{example}[Church encoding of lists]\label{ex:church-list}
The list type over elements of type $A$ is encoded as:
\[
  \mathrm{List}_A = \forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to (A \to \alpha \to \alpha) \to \alpha
\]
The constructors are:
\begin{align*}
  \mathtt{nil} &= \Lambda \alpha.\, \lambda n : \alpha.\, \lambda c : A \to \alpha \to \alpha.\, n \\
  \mathtt{cons} &= \lambda h : A.\, \lambda t : \mathrm{List}_A.\, \Lambda \alpha.\, \lambda n : \alpha.\, \lambda c : A \to \alpha \to \alpha.\, c\; h\; (t\; [\alpha]\; n\; c)
\end{align*}
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Church encoding of natural numbers]\label{ex:church-nat}
The natural numbers are encoded as:
\[
  \mathrm{Nat} = \forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to (\alpha \to \alpha) \to \alpha
\]
The number $n$ is represented as the function that applies its argument $n$ times.
\end{example}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Church encodings of common data types in System F}\label{tab:church-encodings}
\begin{tabular}{lp{6cm}p{4cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Encoding} & \textbf{Key Property} \\
\hline
$\mathrm{Bool}$ & $\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha \to \alpha$ & Case analysis \\
$\mathrm{Nat}$ & $\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to (\alpha \to \alpha) \to \alpha$ & Iteration \\
$\mathrm{List}_A$ & $\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to (A \to \alpha \to \alpha) \to \alpha$ & Fold \\
$\mathrm{Tree}_A$ & $\forall \alpha.\, (A \to \alpha \to \alpha \to \alpha) \to \alpha$ & Tree fold \\
$\exists \alpha.\, \tau$ & $\forall \beta.\, (\forall \alpha.\, \tau \to \beta) \to \beta$ & Existential package \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{The Expressiveness Limitation}\label{sec:expressiveness-limit}

Despite its power, System~F cannot express all computable functions. Since every well-typed term is strongly normalizing, System~F cannot express general recursion. The functions representable in System~F are exactly the \emph{provably total} functions of second-order arithmetic---a large but proper subset of all total computable functions.

\begin{theorem}[Representability]\label{thm:representability}
A function $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is representable in System~F if and only if it is provably total in second-order Peano arithmetic ($\mathrm{PA}_2$).
\end{theorem}

This includes all primitive recursive functions and many beyond (e.g., the Ackermann function), but excludes functions whose totality requires stronger logical principles.

\subsection{Type Inference is Undecidable}\label{sec:inference-undecidable}

\begin{theorem}[Wells, 1999]\label{thm:wells}
Type inference for System~F is undecidable: there is no algorithm that, given an untyped term $e$, determines whether there exists a type $\tau$ such that $\vdash e : \tau$.
\end{theorem}

This result motivates the restriction to rank-1 types (Hindley--Milner) for inference, with explicit annotations for higher-rank polymorphism. The practical impact is that languages like Haskell require type annotations for functions that use higher-rank types, while still inferring rank-1 types automatically.

