\section{Implementations of Polymorphic Type Systems}\label{sec:poly-implementations}

Modern language implementations handle polymorphism through various strategies, each with distinct trade-offs.

\subsection{GHC's Approach: Boxed Values and Specialization}\label{sec:ghc-approach}

GHC compiles polymorphic code using type erasure with boxed representations, then applies aggressive specialization:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Core.} The source program is translated to System~F-like Core, with explicit type abstractions and applications.
  \item \textbf{Erasure.} Type abstractions and applications are erased; all values are boxed (pointers to heap objects).
  \item \textbf{Specialization.} When a polymorphic function is called at a known type, GHC generates a specialized version with unboxed arguments.
  \item \textbf{Worker/Wrapper.} The specialized version (worker) operates on unboxed values; the generic version (wrapper) boxes and unboxes.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source:
  sum :: Num a => [a] -> a
  sum [] = 0
  sum (x:xs) = x + sum xs

  -- After specialization for Int:
  sum_i32 :: [Int] -> Int
  sum_i32 [] = 0#
  sum_i32 (x:xs) = x +# sum_i32 xs   -- uses unboxed addition
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Rust's Approach: Monomorphization}\label{sec:rust-mono}

Rust uses full monomorphization: each instantiation of a generic function generates a separate copy of the code.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  fn max<T: Ord>(a: T, b: T) -> T { if a > b { a } else { b } }

  // Monomorphized:
  fn max_i32(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { if a > b { a } else { b } }
  fn max_f64(a: f64, b: f64) -> f64 { if a > b { a } else { b } }
  fn max_string(a: String, b: String) -> String { ... }
\end{verbatim}

