\subsection{The Boxed/Unboxed Trade-off}\label{sec:boxed-unboxed-tradeoff}

The choice between boxed and unboxed representations is central to polymorphic compilation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Boxed:} Every value is a pointer to a heap object. Uniform representation, but every operation requires indirection.
  \item \textbf{Unboxed:} Values are stored directly in registers/stack. Fast operations, but different types have different sizes.
\end{itemize}

GHC's solution is \emph{worker/wrapper transformation}: the generic function (wrapper) boxes and unboxes values, while the specialized function (worker) operates on unboxed values directly. The optimizer ensures that the wrapper is inlined at call sites where the type is known, eliminating the boxing overhead.

\begin{historical}
The study of System~F's properties has been one of the most fruitful areas in type theory. Girard's strong normalization proof~\cite{girard1972} introduced reducibility candidates, a technique that has been generalized to many other type systems. Reynolds' parametricity theorem~\cite{reynolds1983} revealed that polymorphic functions satisfy strong uniformity properties, leading to the ``theorems for free'' phenomenon. Wells' undecidability result~\cite{wells1999} established the fundamental limit of type inference for polymorphism. The practical implementations---GHC's specialization, Rust's monomorphization---demonstrate that the theoretical insights can be made to work at scale.
\end{historical}

