\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-45}

System~F provides the theoretical foundation for parametric polymorphism. Its key properties---strong normalization, parametricity, and the ability to encode data types---make it an ideal core language for typed functional programming. The extensions to System~F (type operators in F$_\omega$, subtyping in F$_{<:}$, dependent types in the Calculus of Constructions) form a hierarchy of increasingly expressive type systems.

The implementation challenges---type erasure vs.\ monomorphization, rank restrictions, impredicativity---reflect the fundamental tension between expressiveness and decidability that pervades type system design.

For further reading, we recommend Girard's original thesis~\cite{girard1972}, Reynolds' seminal paper~\cite{reynolds1972}, Pierce's textbook~\cite{pierce2002}, and the comprehensive survey by Barendregt~\cite{barendregt1992} on the lambda cube.
